"use strict";(self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith=self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith||[]).push([[27718],{727718:e=>{e.exports='# RFA Reference: Destinations, Webhooks, and Permissions\n\n## Access Request Destination Types\n\n**Key constraints (always include these when listing destination options):**\n\n- **Email** — up to 5 destinations. Does not have to be a Databricks user.\n- **Webhook** (Slack, MS Teams, or custom) — up to 5 destinations. Must be configured in Notification Destinations first.\n- **Redirect URL** — exactly 1 per object. **MUST start with `https://`**. Cannot be combined with email or webhook destinations (exclusive).\n- Email and webhook destinations CAN be combined with each other.\n\n| Type                              | Limit        | Combinable          | Notes                                                                          |\n| --------------------------------- | ------------ | ------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| Email                             | Up to 5      | Yes (with webhooks) | Does not have to be a Databricks user                                          |\n| Webhook (Slack, MS Teams, custom) | Up to 5      | Yes (with emails)   | Must be configured in Notification Destinations first                          |\n| Redirect URL                      | 1 per object | No (exclusive)      | Must start with `https://`. Common targets: ServiceNow, Jira, Opal, Confluence |\n\n## Destination Inheritance\n\nDestinations inherit downward through the UC hierarchy. When a destination is configured at a higher level, it applies to all child objects that don\'t already have their own destination. Lower levels override higher levels.\n\n**Resolution order when a user requests access on a table:**\n\n1. **Object-level explicit destination** — If the table has a destination set, use it.\n2. **Traverse up hierarchy** — If the table has no destination, use the parent schema\'s destination. If the schema has none, check the catalog. Use the first destination encountered.\n\nIf neither step produces a destination, the request cannot be routed via the per-object chain. There is a separate, **independent** workspace-level fallback — see [Default Email Destinations](#default-email-destinations-independent-fallback) below.\n\n> **Note:** metastore-level destinations are not part of the hierarchy walk today. Although the API enumerates `metastore` as a securable type, configuring a destination at the metastore tier for inheritance is future state. Don\'t recommend it as a setup path.\n\n## Default Email Destinations (independent fallback)\n\nThis is a **separate, workspace-level setting** that operates **independently of the per-object resolution chain above**. It is not "step 3" of the chain — it\'s a parallel feature that ensures access requests are deliverable even when no per-object destination is configured.\n\nDatabricks recommends enabling default email destinations as the easiest way to make RFA usable across a workspace without configuring per-object destinations.\n\n**When enabled, requests fall back to:**\n\n| Object Requested                                                                  | Notification Sent To  |\n| --------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------- |\n| Catalog object (catalog, schema, table, view, volume, function, registered model) | Catalog owner\'s email |\n| Object outside a catalog (external location, credential, connection)              | Object owner\'s email  |\n\n**Who can enable it:** A user who is BOTH a metastore admin AND a workspace admin.\n\n**How:** In the workspace, click the profile photo → **Settings** → **Notifications** → turn on **Enable default email destinations for access requests in UC**.\n\nSee [Manage access requests](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/data-governance/unity-catalog/manage-privileges/access-request-destinations#default-destinations) for details.\n\n## Webhook Payload Structure\n\nWhen a request is sent to a webhook destination, the following JSON payload is delivered:\n\n```json\n{\n  "requesterName": "<first-name> <last-name> (<email>)",\n  "objectName": "<catalog>.<schema>.<table>",\n  "objectType": "Table",\n  "privileges": "SELECT",\n  "principalName": "<group-name>",\n  "onBehalfOf": "<group-name>",\n  "onBehalfOfType": "Group",\n  "comment": "My team needs access to run queries on this table.",\n  "databricksWorkspaceUrl": "https://<account>.databricks.com/explore/data/<catalog>/<schema>/<table>?o=<table-id>&activeTab=permissions&showGrantModal=true&requestedPrivileges=SELECT&groupId=<group-id>"\n}\n```\n\n## Common Permission Values\n\n| Permission       | Applies To        | Description                                      |\n| ---------------- | ----------------- | ------------------------------------------------ |\n| `USE_CATALOG`    | catalog           | Required to access any object within the catalog |\n| `USE_SCHEMA`     | schema            | Required to access any object within the schema  |\n| `SELECT`         | table, view       | Read data from the object                        |\n| `MODIFY`         | table             | Insert, update, or delete data                   |\n| `CREATE_TABLE`   | schema            | Create tables within the schema                  |\n| `CREATE_SCHEMA`  | catalog           | Create schemas within the catalog                |\n| `ALL_PRIVILEGES` | any               | Full access to the object                        |\n| `MANAGE`         | any               | Full control including permission management     |\n| `BROWSE`         | catalog           | Discover metadata of objects within the catalog  |\n| `READ_FILES`     | external_location | Read files from the external location            |\n| `WRITE_FILES`    | external_location | Write files to the external location             |\n| `EXECUTE`        | function          | Execute a function or registered model           |\n\n## Product Surfaces That Trigger RFA\n\nUsers can request access from these surfaces:\n\n- **Direct URL** — Navigating to an object\'s Catalog Explorer page they don\'t have access to\n- **Catalog Explorer** — Browsing objects with the BROWSE privilege and requesting additional permissions\n- **SQL Editor & Notebooks** — Encountering permission errors while authoring queries\n- **AI/BI Genie & Dashboards** — Blocked from using a feature due to missing data access\n\n## Approval Workflow\n\nWhen an approver receives a request (via email, Slack, or webhook), they can:\n\n1. Click "Open permission settings" to validate identity via Databricks sign-in\n2. Choose one of:\n   - **Add principal to group(s)** — Add the requester to existing groups that have the needed privileges\n   - **Grant privileges to principal** — Grant access directly, optionally using presets like "Data Reader"\n\n## API Quick Reference\n\n### Submit access request\n\n```\nPOST /api/3.0/rfa/requests\n```\n\n### Check destinations\n\n```\nGET /api/3.0/rfa/destinations/{securable_type}/{full_name}\n```\n\n### Update destinations (requires MANAGE or object ownership)\n\n```\nPUT /api/3.0/rfa/destinations\n```\n\n### CLI equivalents\n\n```bash\ndatabricks rfa batch-create-access-requests --json \'{...}\'\ndatabricks rfa get-access-request-destinations {securable_type} {full_name}\n```\n\n## Limitations\n\n- Principals must be unique within a single batch API call\n- No time-based or expiring access — approved access is permanent until revoked\n- No in-product request status tracking — access requests are tracked via audit logs only\n- Group membership changes in Databricks do NOT sync back to the customer\'s IDP\n- Not available in Mooncake or AWS GovCloud regions\n\n> Note: requesting access to multiple distinct objects in a single call **is supported** — `POST /api/3.0/rfa/requests` accepts a `requests` array of independent items targeting different securables. See [batchCreateAccessRequests](https://docs.databricks.com/api/workspace/rfa/batchcreateaccessrequests).\n\n## Destination Visibility\n\nWho can see the destinations returned by `GET /api/3.0/rfa/destinations/{securable_type}/{full_name}` depends on the destination type and the caller\'s permissions:\n\n| Destination Type / Source                                     | Who Can See It                                           |\n| ------------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Redirect URL destination (any securable)                      | Any authenticated caller                                 |\n| Destination set on a securable (catalog, schema, table, etc.) | Only callers with `BROWSE` (or higher) on that securable |\n\n**Implication for empty responses:** An empty `destinations` array can mean either (a) no destination is configured for this securable or any of its ancestors, OR (b) the caller lacks `BROWSE` permission on the securable so non-public destinations are filtered out. The API does not distinguish these cases. See [getAccessRequestDestinations](https://docs.databricks.com/api/workspace/rfa/getaccessrequestdestinations).\n'}}]);
